[38;2;124;124;124malgebra R over a field of characteristic 0, and R is Cohen-Macaulay, then R^G is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mCohen-Macaulay.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is the Hochster-Roberts theorem. Let me recall the proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Hochster-Roberts theorem (1974) states: If G is a linearly reductive group [0m
[38;2;124;124;124macting on a regular ring (or more generally, if R is Cohen-Macaulay and G is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlinearly reductive), then R^G is Cohen-Macaulay.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn characteristic 0, every finite group is linearly reductive (Maschke's [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem). So the result follows from the Hochster-Roberts theorem.[0m

